                <pb facs="0231" n="141" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg141"/>

                <head>No. 100.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Williamson to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation in Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">San Salvador</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1874-12-03"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 3, 1874</date>. (Rec’d Jan. 13, 1875.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 275.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that there seems
                    to be no doubt now that President Leiva has been elected constitutional
                    President of Honduras by a large majority. My information, from reliable
                    sources, is that the election was unattended by those restraints put upon voters
                    sometimes in Central America when the chief of the state or his friend is a
                    candidate for the presidency. I understand, and am prepared to believe, from my
                    knowledge of President Leiva’s character, that the election was a fair one in
                    all respects, and that his elevation to the constitutional presidency of
                    Honduras is the result of a well-deserved popularity.</p>
                <p>He and President Gonzalez, of this State, seem to appreciate and practice
                    republican principles in a manner worthy of imitation by many of the rulers of
                    the so-called Spanish republics of this hemisphere.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. WILLIAMSON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
